; pi4FpState.pi4 - STAGE 0 of the hardware floating-point work. Read the
;                   machine's actual FP trap configuration and print it.
;                   Execute NO floating-point instruction. Change nothing.
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship.
;
;   pmfc pi4FpState.pi4 -t pi4 --load-addr 0x400000 \
;        --stack-addr 0x3000000 --entry-returns -o fpstate.img
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHY THIS EXISTS, AND WHY IT COMES BEFORE ANY OTHER FP WORK
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; The Cortex-A72 has a full FP and Advanced SIMD unit and this backend
; does not emit a single FP instruction. The obvious next move is to
; emit one. The reason that is the WRONG next move is that FP access at
; EL2 is gated by CPTR_EL2.TFP, and if that bit is set the first `fmul`
; does not compute anything - it raises a Trapped-FP-access exception
; to EL2.
;
; This program is entered at EL2 and NOTHING IN THIS TREE INSTALLS AN
; EXCEPTION VECTOR. Checked mechanically on 2026-08-26: a grep for
; `vbar` across the whole RaspberryPi4 tree finds the assembler's
; encoding table, the emulator's decode table and one comment in
; mmu.pi4 listing register names - and no writer anywhere. VBAR_EL2 is
; therefore whatever reset left it at, so an exception taken at EL2
; branches into an address nobody chose.
;
; The observable result of that is A HANG WITH NO CONSOLE OUTPUT. Not a
; fault message, not a wrong number - silence. That failure looks
; identical to a dozen other failures (a bad load address, a stack in
; the wrong place, a UART that never initialised), so a first attempt
; that hangs would burn a session on the wrong hypothesis.
;
; This probe removes that ambiguity BEFORE any FP instruction is
; assembled, by reading the gate register and printing it. It is free,
; it cannot hang, and its answer decides whether the emitter has to
; write CPTR_EL2 in the program prologue or merely check it.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THIS IS SAFE, AND HERE IS THE ARGUMENT RATHER THAN THE ASSERTION
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Every instruction this probe adds over an empty program is an `mrs`.
; MRS reads. None of the five registers read here can fault at EL2, no
; write is performed to any of them, and there is no floating-point
; instruction anywhere in this file - which is the specific hazard the
; probe exists to measure and therefore the specific thing it must not
; contain. There is no sequence here for which a power cycle could be
; needed.
;
; ARM THE DEADMAN ANYWAY, exactly as pi4MmuState.pi4 does and for the
; same reason: the habit is the thing being maintained, not the
; coverage. It costs one line.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT THE OUTPUT MEANS - read this BEFORE running it, not after
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; CPTR_EL2 is the line that matters, and specifically BIT 10.
;
; The architectural rule, quoted rather than recalled. The shared
; pseudocode in Datasheets/arm-a64-instruction-set.txt:479887-479895
; (AArch64_CheckFPAdvSIMDTrap) reads, for an execution at EL2 that is
; not in a VHE host:
;
;     if PSTATE.EL IN {EL0, EL1, EL2} && EL2Enabled() then
;         if ELIsInHost(EL2) then  ... FPEN form ...
;         else
;             if CPTR_EL2().TFP == '1' then
;                 AArch64_AdvSIMDFPAccessTrap(EL2);
;             end;
;         end;
;     end;
;
; and the CPACR_EL1 check that precedes it, at :479919-479929, applies
; only when PSTATE.EL is EL0 or EL1. So at EL2, CPACR_EL1 is
; IRRELEVANT and CPTR_EL2.TFP is the whole gate. That matters because
; CPACR_EL1 is the register every ARM bring-up note talks about, and
; writing it here would accomplish nothing at all while looking exactly
; like a fix.
;
; BIT 10 CLEAR   FP and SIMD are usable at EL2 as the machine stands.
;                An `fmul s0, s0, s1` will compute.
;
; BIT 10 SET     FP is TRAPPED. Every FP instruction becomes an
;                exception into an uninstalled vector, i.e. a silent
;                hang. The emitter must clear the bit before the first
;                FP instruction in any program that uses one.
;
; WHAT THE FIRMWARE LEAVES BEHIND, from the source rather than from
; memory. Datasheets/pi4/armstub8.S is the EL3 stub that runs before
; us. At :115-117 it writes
;
;     /* Enable FP/SIMD */
;     /* Bit 10 (TFP) is set to 0 */
;     msr CPTR_EL3, xzr
;
; so the EL3 gate is open. It then writes SCTLR_EL2 at :136-141 and
; erets to EL2h at :143-147. IT NEVER WRITES CPTR_EL2. Read the whole
; file if you doubt it - it is 240 lines and `cptr` appears once.
;
; So CPTR_EL2 arrives at our code AT ITS RESET VALUE, and no document
; on this machine states what that value is. The architecture makes
; most of the register's bits UNKNOWN at reset; the Cortex-A72 TRM
; would say what this part actually does, and the A72 TRM is not on
; this machine - see THE MISSING DOCUMENTS at the foot of this file.
;
; THAT UNCERTAINTY IS PRECISELY WHY THIS PROBE EXISTS. Measure it.
;
; HCR_EL2 bit 34 (E2H) is the second line that matters, and it decides
; how to READ the first one. CPTR_EL2 has two entirely different
; layouts:
;
;     E2H == 0   the plain layout. Bit 10 is TFP, and TFP SET means
;                trapped. Writing $33FF - all the RES1 bits and nothing
;                else - opens the gate.
;
;     E2H == 1   the VHE layout. Bits 21:20 are FPEN and the polarity
;                is INVERTED: 00 means DISABLED and 11 means enabled.
;                Writing $33FF under this layout would leave FPEN at 00
;                and TRAP EVERY FP INSTRUCTION - the exact bug this
;                probe is here to prevent, wearing the costume of the
;                fix.
;
; The Cortex-A72 is an ARMv8.0-A part and does not implement FEAT_VHE,
; so E2H should read 0 and be RES0. armstub8.S never writes HCR_EL2 at
; all, which is consistent. Both of those are expectations; the number
; printed below is the measurement.
;
; BOTH QUESTIONS ARE ASKED BELOW, because they are different questions.
; HCR_EL2.E2H says what the bit IS right now. ID_AA64MMFR1_EL1.VH, bits
; 11:8, says whether the silicon can ever SET it - 0b0000 is NI - and
; that register's encoding (3,0,0,7,1) is at
; RaspberryPi4/Reference/llvm19.1.0_AArch64SystemOperands.td:757. If VH
; reads NI then E2H is RES0 and the plain CPTR_EL2 layout is not merely
; what is in force today, it is the only layout this part has. That is
; the stronger answer and it is the one the emitter needs, because the
; prologue has to be right on every boot and not only on the boot that
; happened to be measured.
;
; ID_AA64PFR0_EL1 answers the prior question nobody has actually asked
; on this board: IS THERE A FLOATING-POINT UNIT? Field FP at bits 19:16
; reads 0b0000 for implemented and 0b1111 for not; field AdvSIMD at
; 23:20 says the same for the SIMD half. Every FP instruction page
; carries "if !IsFeatureImplemented(FEAT_FP) then
; EndOfDecode(Decode_UNDEF)", so on a part without the unit an `fmul` is
; not slow - it is UNDEFINED, which is another exception into the same
; uninstalled vector and another silent hang wearing the same face as a
; trap. The unit is mandatory on an ARMv8-A A-profile core and will be
; there; asking costs one instruction and turns an assumption into a
; reading.
;
; MIDR_EL1 is printed to identify the part, because every sentence
; above about reset values and VHE is a sentence about a Cortex-A72
; specifically. The A72's MIDR part number is $D08 in bits 15:4. If a
; future board reports something else, none of the reasoning above has
; been checked against it.
;
; SCTLR_EL2 is printed because it costs one line and because every
; other stage-0 probe in this directory prints it, so the outputs stay
; comparable. $30C50830 is the value armstub8.S:136-141 writes.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT THIS PROBE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; IT DOES NOT WRITE CPTR_EL2. A stage-0 probe measures. Clearing the
; trap is a change to machine state that the monitor has to survive
; being returned to, and it belongs in the emitter's prologue where
; every program gets it consistently, not in a diagnostic where it
; would be a one-off that nothing else inherits.
;
; IT DOES NOT EXECUTE AN FP INSTRUCTION. Obviously. The whole point is
; to find out whether doing so is safe.
;
; IT READS FPCR LAST, AND ONLY IF THE TRAP IS OPEN. This is the one
; ordering constraint in the file and it is not decoration.
;
; FPCR is a floating-point register. An access to it is a floating-point
; access, and the architecture traps floating-point accesses through the
; same CPTR_EL2.TFP this probe exists to measure. If TFP is set, then
; `mrs x0, fpcr` is not a read that returns a surprising number - it is
; an exception into the uninstalled vector, and the probe hangs doing
; the very thing it was written to make unnecessary. So CPTR_EL2 is read
; first, TFP is tested, and FPCR is read only when the answer is that it
; is safe.
;
; THAT PARTICULAR TRAP RULE IS NOT CITED, and the honesty is deliberate.
; The MRS page (arm-a64-instruction-set.txt:43633) delegates to
; AArch64_SysRegRead, whose per-register trap behaviour lives in the
; System Register Descriptions volume, which is not on this machine. So
; the ordering above is a PRECAUTION reasoned from what FPCR is, not a
; quotation. It costs one branch. If the rule turns out not to apply,
; nothing has been lost; if it does apply and the guard were missing,
; the cost is a session spent debugging the diagnostic.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT IS STILL MISSING, AFTER THIS PROBE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; The four registers this work needed are now encodable - FPCR, FPSR,
; ID_AA64PFR0_EL1 and ID_AA64MMFR1_EL1 were added to A64SystemRegBase()
; on 2026-08-26, cited to
; RaspberryPi4/Reference/llvm19.1.0_AArch64SystemOperands.td.
;
; WHAT THAT TABLE DOES NOT GIVE IS FPCR's FIELD LAYOUT, and that is the
; remaining gap. It is a table of (name, op0, op1, CRn, CRm, op2): it
; says where FPCR lives and not which bit of it is which. Three of its
; fields change ANSWERS rather than speed:
;
;   FZ    flushes subnormal inputs and results to zero. The shared
;         pseudocode reads it at
;         arm-a64-instruction-set.txt:507197 (`let fz : boolean =
;         fpcr.FZ == '1' && ...` inside FPUnpackBase).
;   DN    replaces a propagated NaN with the default NaN.
;         arm-a64-instruction-set.txt:505977 (`if fpcr.DN == '1' then
;         result = FPDefaultNaN{N}(fpcr)` inside FPProcessNaN).
;   RMode selects the rounding mode.
;
; The pseudocode names all three and states the position of none of
; them. A correctly-rounded software core computes as though all three
; were zero, so if the part boots with FZ set then every subnormal
; answer differs from software and nothing says so.
;
; SO THIS PROBE PRINTS FPCR RAW AND DECODES NOTHING FROM IT. That is the
; same discipline mmu.pi4 applies to SCTLR_EL2 and for the same reason:
; a raw hex value is a fact, and a decode built on a recalled bit
; position is a fact-shaped guess wearing the same clothes. If the value
; printed is zero, every field is zero whatever the layout is, and the
; question stops mattering. If it is not zero, the layout has to be
; obtained before anything is written back - see THE MISSING DOCUMENTS.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE MISSING DOCUMENTS - named exactly, so nobody has to go looking
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; TWO documents would close what is left, and neither is on this machine.
;
;   1. FPCR's FIELD LAYOUT - the positions of DN, FZ and RMode.
;      Authoritative source: Arm DDI 0487, the A-profile Architecture
;      Reference Manual, in the System Register Descriptions volume.
;      That manual is roughly 14,000 pages behind a site that will not
;      serve a PDF to a fetch, which is why this repo cites vendor
;      tables instead - and this is the one question no vendor TABLE can
;      answer, because a table of encodings is not a table of fields.
;      A smaller fetchable source that does carry the positions would
;      also do; it has to be a real source, not a remembered constant.
;
;      HOW BADLY IT IS NEEDED DEPENDS ON WHAT THIS PROBE PRINTS. If
;      FPCR reads as zero on the board, the machine is already in the
;      state a correctly-rounded software core assumes, and the layout
;      is needed only to WRITE the register defensively rather than to
;      understand it. If it reads non-zero, nothing can proceed until
;      the layout is in hand.
;
;   2. The Cortex-A72 Technical Reference Manual, which would state the
;      RESET VALUES of CPTR_EL2 and FPCR for this specific part. The
;      architecture declines to define them, so only the implementer's
;      manual knows.
;
;      THIS PROBE STANDS IN FOR THAT ONE, and is arguably the better
;      source: it reports what the board in the room actually does, on
;      the firmware that is actually on the card, rather than what the
;      part is documented to do. Fetch the TRM only if a number printed
;      below is surprising and needs explaining.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/safety.pi4"

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The reads. One `mrs` each, in an ASM block, because this language has
;  no syntax for a system register and the assembler does have the
;  encoding. The idiom - ASM, one mrs into x0, fall out of the block,
;  ProcedureReturn - is timer.pi4:198-214's and mmu.pi4:585-588's and is
;  not invented here.
;
;  WHERE EACH ENCODING COMES FROM, so that a wrong number printed below
;  can be traced to a table rather than to a guess:
;    currentel          A64Assembler.pbi:446   hand-written, pre-dates the table
;    midr_el1           A64Assembler.pbi:710   v6.12_arm64_sysreg.h:278
;    sctlr_el2          A64Assembler.pbi:475   hand-written 2026-08-24
;    hcr_el2            A64Assembler.pbi:476   hand-written 2026-08-24
;    cptr_el2           A64Assembler.pbi:610   v6.12_arm64_sysreg.h:512
;    fpcr               A64Assembler.pbi       llvm19.1.0_AArch64SystemOperands.td:990
;    fpsr               A64Assembler.pbi       llvm19.1.0_AArch64SystemOperands.td:991
;    id_aa64pfr0_el1    A64Assembler.pbi       llvm19.1.0_AArch64SystemOperands.td:744
;    id_aa64mmfr1_el1   A64Assembler.pbi       llvm19.1.0_AArch64SystemOperands.td:757
;
;  cptr_el2 being present before this work started is worth naming: it
;  arrived with the generated table, from v6.12_arm64_sysreg.h:512, and
;  its value $D51C1140 is independently reproduced by
;  llvm19.1.0_AArch64SystemOperands.td:951, which gives the same
;  (3,4,1,1,2). Two unrelated vendor tables agreeing on the encoding of
;  the one register this whole probe turns on.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i FpCurrentEl()
  ASM
    mrs x0, currentel
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i FpMidrEl1()
  ASM
    mrs x0, midr_el1
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i FpSctlrEl2()
  ASM
    mrs x0, sctlr_el2
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i FpHcrEl2()
  ASM
    mrs x0, hcr_el2
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i FpCptrEl2()
  ASM
    mrs x0, cptr_el2
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; The two feature-identification registers. Reading either is an
; ordinary system-register read and is not a floating-point access, so
; these are safe to call before the trap has been established - which is
; the order Main() uses them in.
Procedure.i FpIdPfr0()
  ASM
    mrs x0, id_aa64pfr0_el1
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i FpIdMmfr1()
  ASM
    mrs x0, id_aa64mmfr1_el1
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THESE TWO ARE THE DANGEROUS ONES. DO NOT CALL THEM UNCONDITIONALLY.
;
;  FPCR and FPSR are floating-point registers, so an access to either is
;  a floating-point access and is subject to the same CPTR_EL2.TFP this
;  probe exists to measure. Calling one on a machine where the trap is
;  closed takes an exception into an uninstalled vector, and the probe
;  hangs with no output - which is precisely the failure it was written
;  to make impossible to mistake for anything else.
;
;  Main() reads CPTR_EL2 first and calls these only when bit 10 is
;  clear. The reasoning behind that ordering, and the admission that the
;  trap rule for FPCR specifically is NOT quotable from any document on
;  this machine, is in the header above under WHAT THIS PROBE
;  DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i FpFpcr()
  ASM
    mrs x0, fpcr
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i FpFpsr()
  ASM
    mrs x0, fpsr
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PutHex16 - a full 64-bit value, all sixteen digits, always.
;
;  Copied deliberately from pi4MmuState.pi4 rather than shared, because
;  a diagnostic that depends on another diagnostic is a diagnostic that
;  stops building when somebody tidies the other one. The reasoning for
;  sixteen digits with no zero suppression is that file's and still
;  holds: "0000000030C50830" says the top half was read and was zero,
;  and "30C50830" leaves open the possibility that only 32 bits were
;  fetched.
;
;  No character literals - house rule. 48 is the code for the digit
;  zero and 55 is the code for the letter A minus ten.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure PutHex16(v.i)
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  For i = 15 To 0 Step -1
    n = (v >> (i * 4)) & $F
    If n < 10
      UartWrite(48 + n)
    Else
      UartWrite(55 + n)
    EndIf
  Next
EndProcedure

Procedure Row(*label, v.i)
  UartWriteStr(*label)
  UartWrite(36)                 ; the dollar sign that marks hex here
  PutHex16(v)
  UartWriteNl()
EndProcedure

Procedure Verdict(*label, *text)
  UartWriteStr(*label)
  UartWriteStr(*text)
  UartWriteNl()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Main()
  Define cptr.i
  Define hcr.i
  Define midr.i
  Define pfr0.i
  Define mmfr1.i
  Define tfp.i
  Define e2h.i
  Define vh.i
  Define fpfield.i
  Define simdfield.i
  Define part.i

  UartInit(115200)
  SafetyWatchdogStart(15)

  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("pi4FpState - stage 0, read only, no FP instruction")
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteNl()

  ; --- everything that is safe to read unconditionally ---------------
  ; None of these is a floating-point access, so none of them can be
  ; caught by the trap being measured. CPTR_EL2 is read HERE, before
  ; anything else needs it, because two later decisions depend on it:
  ; the verdict, and whether FPCR may be touched at all.
  midr  = FpMidrEl1()
  cptr  = FpCptrEl2()
  hcr   = FpHcrEl2()
  pfr0  = FpIdPfr0()
  mmfr1 = FpIdMmfr1()

  Row("CurrentEL raw   ", FpCurrentEl())
  Row("MIDR_EL1        ", midr)
  Row("SCTLR_EL2       ", FpSctlrEl2())
  Row("HCR_EL2         ", hcr)
  Row("CPTR_EL2        ", cptr)
  Row("ID_AA64PFR0_EL1 ", pfr0)
  Row("ID_AA64MMFR1_EL1", mmfr1)

  UartWriteNl()

  ; --- the decoded fields, each printed as its own row ---------------
  ;
  ; DECODED HERE AND NOT IN mmu.pi4's style, and the difference is
  ; deliberate. That file refuses to decode SCTLR_EL2 because no
  ; document on this machine states its bit positions. These positions
  ; ARE stated by documents on this machine, so decoding them is
  ; reporting a fact rather than dressing up a guess:
  ;
  ;   CPTR_EL2 bit 10 is TFP. u-boot's own header at
  ;   RaspberryPi4/Reference/v2025.01_arm_system.h:54 defines
  ;   CPTR_EL2_RES1 as (3 << 12 | 0x3ff), which is $33FF - bits 13, 12
  ;   and 9 down to 0. Bit 10 is conspicuously NOT in the RES1 set,
  ;   which is exactly what you would expect of the one writable trap
  ;   bit in that range, and the pseudocode quoted in this file's header
  ;   names CPTR_EL2.TFP as the gate.
  ;
  ;   ID_AA64PFR0_EL1 field FP is bits 19:16 and field AdvSIMD is bits
  ;   23:20, both on a scale where 0b0000 means implemented and 0b1111
  ;   means not. Read out of the kernel's own field table on 2026-08-26
  ;   ("SignedEnum 19:16 FP / 0b0000 IMP / 0b1111 NI").
  ;
  ;   ID_AA64MMFR1_EL1 field VH is bits 11:8, 0b0000 meaning FEAT_VHE is
  ;   not implemented, from the same table.
  ;
  ;   HCR_EL2 bit 34 is E2H. This is the one position here that no file
  ;   on this machine states outright, so it is stated as an ASSUMPTION
  ;   and the raw HCR_EL2 is printed above regardless. If the raw value
  ;   is zero the assumption cannot change the verdict, because every
  ;   bit is clear - and if VH says FEAT_VHE is absent then E2H is RES0
  ;   and the question is settled from the other direction as well.
  ;
  ;   MIDR_EL1 bits 15:4 are the part number. $D08 is the Cortex-A72.
  part      = (midr >> 4) & $FFF
  tfp       = (cptr >> 10) & 1
  e2h       = (hcr >> 34) & 1
  vh        = (mmfr1 >> 8) & $F
  fpfield   = (pfr0 >> 16) & $F
  simdfield = (pfr0 >> 20) & $F

  Row("part number     ", part)
  Row("PFR0 FP field   ", fpfield)
  Row("PFR0 AdvSIMD    ", simdfield)
  Row("MMFR1 VH field  ", vh)
  Row("CPTR_EL2 bit 10 ", tfp)
  Row("HCR_EL2  bit 34 ", e2h)

  UartWriteNl()

  ; --- is there a unit at all -----------------------------------------
  ; Asked before the trap question, because "trapped" and "absent" have
  ; the same symptom - an exception into a vector nobody installed - and
  ; only this register can tell them apart.
  If fpfield = $F
    Verdict("VERDICT: ", "THIS PART REPORTS NO FLOATING-POINT UNIT.")
    Verdict("         ", "ID_AA64PFR0_EL1.FP reads 0b1111, which is NI.")
    Verdict("         ", "Every FP instruction is UNDEFINED here, not")
    Verdict("         ", "merely trapped, and no CPTR_EL2 setting can")
    Verdict("         ", "change that. Stop: the whole plan assumes a")
    Verdict("         ", "unit that this reading says is not present.")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartDrain()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; --- the verdict, spelled out --------------------------------------
  ;
  ; The numbers above are the finding; this is the convenience. It is
  ; written out so that nobody at the bench has to compare two
  ; sixteen-digit numbers by eye at the end of a long session.
  ;
  ; THE E2H TEST COMES FIRST because it decides what bit 10 even means.
  ; A verdict on TFP that was reached without checking the layout is
  ; not a verdict, it is a coin toss that happened to land right.
  If e2h = 1
    Verdict("VERDICT: ", "HCR_EL2.E2H is SET. CPTR_EL2 is in its VHE")
    Verdict("         ", "layout and bit 10 is NOT TFP. Nothing below")
    Verdict("         ", "applies. Do not write $33FF - under this")
    Verdict("         ", "layout that clears FPEN and traps everything.")
    Verdict("         ", "Stop and re-derive from the VHE field table.")
  Else
    If tfp = 0
      Verdict("VERDICT: ", "FP and SIMD are USABLE at EL2 right now.")
      Verdict("         ", "CPTR_EL2.TFP is clear, so an fmul computes")
      Verdict("         ", "rather than trapping. The emitter should")
      Verdict("         ", "STILL write the register, because this is")
      Verdict("         ", "one board on one firmware and a reset value")
      Verdict("         ", "no document states is not a guarantee.")
    Else
      Verdict("VERDICT: ", "FP IS TRAPPED. CPTR_EL2.TFP is set, so the")
      Verdict("         ", "first fmul in any program would take an")
      Verdict("         ", "exception into an uninstalled EL2 vector,")
      Verdict("         ", "which presents as a hang with no output.")
      Verdict("         ", "The emitter MUST clear it before emitting")
      Verdict("         ", "any FP instruction. This is the finding the")
      Verdict("         ", "probe exists for.")
    EndIf
  EndIf

  UartWriteNl()

  ; --- FPCR, and ONLY if the trap is open ------------------------------
  ; See the header. FPCR is a floating-point register, so reading it is
  ; a floating-point access; on a machine with TFP set this read is the
  ; hang. The guard is the whole reason CPTR_EL2 was read first.
  ;
  ; NOTHING IS DECODED FROM THE VALUE. No document on this machine
  ; states where DN, FZ or RMode sit inside FPCR, so the raw sixteen
  ; digits are the honest output and a decode would be an invention.
  ; What can be said without the layout is said: if the value is zero,
  ; every field is zero whatever the layout, which is exactly the state
  ; a correctly-rounded software implementation assumes.
  If tfp = 0
    Define fpcr.i
    Define fpsr.i
    fpcr = FpFpcr()
    fpsr = FpFpsr()
    Row("FPCR            ", fpcr)
    Row("FPSR            ", fpsr)
    UartWriteNl()
    If fpcr = 0
      Verdict("FPCR:    ", "zero. Every field is zero whatever the layout")
      Verdict("         ", "is, so rounding is nearest-even, subnormals")
      Verdict("         ", "are not flushed and NaN payloads propagate.")
      Verdict("         ", "That is the state a correctly-rounded software")
      Verdict("         ", "core assumes, so the differential gate can be")
      Verdict("         ", "run without obtaining the field layout first.")
    Else
      Verdict("FPCR:    ", "NOT zero. Some field is set and this file")
      Verdict("         ", "deliberately does not know which. Obtain the")
      Verdict("         ", "layout from a real source before writing this")
      Verdict("         ", "register or trusting a bit-exactness result -")
      Verdict("         ", "FZ, DN and RMode each change ANSWERS. See THE")
      Verdict("         ", "MISSING DOCUMENTS in this file's header.")
    EndIf
    UartWriteNl()
  Else
    Verdict("FPCR:    ", "NOT READ. CPTR_EL2.TFP is set, and an access")
    Verdict("         ", "to FPCR is itself a floating-point access, so")
    Verdict("         ", "reading it here would have hung this probe.")
    UartWriteNl()
  EndIf

  ; --- the one remaining note ------------------------------------------
  ; VH is reported separately from the E2H verdict because they answer
  ; different questions and only the pair is conclusive: E2H is what the
  ; bit is, VH is whether it could ever be anything else.
  If vh = 0
    Verdict("NOTE:    ", "MMFR1.VH is 0, so FEAT_VHE is not implemented")
    Verdict("         ", "and HCR_EL2.E2H is RES0. The plain CPTR_EL2")
    Verdict("         ", "layout is the only one this part has, on every")
    Verdict("         ", "boot and not merely on this one.")
    UartWriteNl()
  Else
    Verdict("NOTE:    ", "MMFR1.VH is NOT 0 - this part implements")
    Verdict("         ", "FEAT_VHE, so E2H is writable and the CPTR_EL2")
    Verdict("         ", "layout can change under software. The emitter")
    Verdict("         ", "must read E2H rather than assume it.")
    UartWriteNl()
  EndIf

  If part <> $D08
    Verdict("NOTE:    ", "MIDR part number is not $D08, so this is not")
    Verdict("         ", "a Cortex-A72 and none of the reasoning in this")
    Verdict("         ", "file's header has been checked against it.")
    UartWriteNl()
  EndIf

  UartDrain()

  ; Back to the monitor. Zero is the boring answer and the one the
  ; monitor prints as the payload's return value; the finding is in the
  ; text above, not in this number.
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
